WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Run your Python shelf script and save its native results.
Why: Keep the source inputs, inspect native fields, and save a complete result package.
Look for: Have run.py, validate_dataset.py and icepack-shelf-small-v1.json ready.

2
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Start here in CryoLauncher. Choose Cloud for this tutorial.
Why: Your workspace keeps scripts, dataset references and results together.
Look for: Your workspace keeps scripts, datasets, run history and results together.

3
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Choose Cloud → Advanced → Icepack.
Why: Choose the intended interface, model and example before editing or launching a run.
Look for: Use a separate editable workspace for your script.

4
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Enter a new name and choose New example.
Why: An owned example stores your files separately from the supplied application source.
Look for: Create an owned workspace example for this experiment.

5
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Wait for the created-workspace message.
Why: An owned example stores your files separately from the supplied application source.
Look for: This example is pilot-icepack-script-1118.

6
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Choose run.py and validate_dataset.py in your file picker; Open.
Why: Selecting local files prepares them for import; importing saves them in this example.
Look for: Keep the script and validator together.

7
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Check the script picker changes from zero to two files.
Why: Selecting local files prepares them for import; importing saves them in this example.
Look for: Selected files are not saved until you import them.

8
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Choose Import script files.
Why: Import saves the selected source files in this example; it does not launch the model.
Look for: Enable Replace existing script files if run.py already exists.

9
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:41.000
Check Imported run.py, validate_dataset.py. Files are saved.
Why: Import saves the selected source files in this example; it does not launch the model.
Look for: The file selector now contains the runnable script.

10
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Set the Run target to run.py.
Why: Run target selects the entry script; other imported files can act as helpers.
Look for: The validator checks the JSON before loading the scientific libraries.

11
00:01:51.000 --> 00:02:02.000
In Datasets select icepack-shelf-small-v1.json.
Why: Select the dataset that this script will reference and read at launch.
Look for: If it is absent, choose the data file and Upload it first.

12
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Choose Reference in example.
Why: A reference stages this dataset under data/ when the selected example launches.
Look for: Use the supplied JSON filename as the reference alias.

13
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Confirm the reference saved in this example.
Why: A reference stages this dataset under data/ when the selected example launches.
Look for: The script reads data/icepack-shelf-small-v1.json.

14
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Review the short 10 km × 10 km CG1 shelf experiment.
Why: The unchanged saved figure preserves the complete map, axes and colour scale.
Look for: One 0.01-year step; temperature 253.15 K; zero accumulation.

15
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:43.000
Choose Review & Launch.
Why: Review confirms the intended model, source, resources and charges before submission.
Look for: Check the owned example, run.py target and resources.

16
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:54.000
Control guide: confirm Launch cloud run when checks are Ready.
Why: This confirmation stages the reviewed inputs and starts a paid cloud job.
Look for: The following card belongs to this tutorial’s original native run.

17
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Follow the new custom-script run card.
Why: Submission, queue/startup and native model execution are separate stages.
Look for: The native job used 2 vCPU and 8 GiB.

18
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:11.000
Open the completed native results.
Why: A completed native job and its recorded provenance provide execution evidence.
Look for: Submission → start: 220.087 s; task: 48.192 s; script: 15.6633 s.

19
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Select a field and use Render when needed.
Why: The solution, field name, units and mesh counts identify what you are viewing.
Look for: The first field may preview automatically.

20
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Inspect the native field summary.
Why: The solution, field name, units and mesh counts identify what you are viewing.
Look for: 25 vertices / 32 triangles; finite CG1 thickness and velocity.

21
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Open the full saved thickness figure.
Why: The unchanged saved figure preserves the complete map, axes and colour scale.
Look for: Area-weighted mean thickness: 400.006923 m.

22
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Inspect speed and direction in the saved figure.
Why: The unchanged saved figure preserves the complete map, axes and colour scale.
Look for: Domain RMS speed: 130.141951 m/year; arrows show direction.

23
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:02.000
Choose Download results.
Why: The archive keeps this run’s numeric outputs, figures and provenance together.
Look for: Wait for the fresh HTTPS archive link.

24
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Open the new link and save the 11-member ZIP.
Why: The archive keeps this run’s numeric outputs, figures and provenance together.
Look for: Keep it with your script, validator and JSON.

25
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:26.000
Keep this short fixture’s scientific scope with the archive.
Why: Keep the scientific input definitions and numerical qualifications with the result.
Look for: The 100→200 m/year initial guess is not an imposed outflow speed.
